        "title": "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "cb68c199-4d27-5eba-880f-baef007fae92",
        ":question/problem": "For $g(x)=\\ln(2 x)$, find $g^{′}(x).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\ln u,\\,u=2 x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} &=\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (2) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 x}\\cdot (2) \\\\ &=\\frac{2}{2 x} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{2}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
